WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:02.860
Remaking a classic movie is never easy.

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:06.640
You've got to appeal to fans of the original
while impressing a whole new crop of critics

00:00:06.649 --> 00:00:08.620
and drawing in new fans at the same time.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.000
It's a tough balancing act... and it's easy
to screw up.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:17.560
In the 1971 British crime film Get Carter,
London gangster Jack Carter returns to his

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.360
hometown after the death of his brother.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.220
When he suspects foul play, he decides to
dig deeper and find out who was really responsible.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:28.660
As he delves back into the city's world of
organized crime, tensions escalate and violent

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:29.980
conflict ensues.

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:34.910
The 2000 remake of Get Carter, starring Sylvester
Stallone, wasn't exactly a bright spot in

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:35.860
the actor's career.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:40.510
This time, the story was set in America, with
Carter a mob enforcer living in Las Vegas

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:43.000
who returns home to Seattle after his brother's
death.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:47.600
But it wasn't the change in setting that disappointed
critics it was the fact that the plot was

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.120
formulaic and contrived in comparison to the
original, despite the fact that it was supposed

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:53.720
to be a suspenseful mystery.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.160
Entertainment Weekly's Owen Gleiberman wrote:

00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:00.400
"Carter, who goes to Seattle to hunt down
his brother's killer, may have entered a labyrinth

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.960
of evil, but his response to it [...] is as
hollow as it is monolithic."

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:09.900
1990's Total Recall follows Douglas Quaid,
a construction worker who begins having disturbing

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.450
dreams about life on Mars.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:15.900
He ends up getting a memory chip implanted
that gives him a virtual reality experience

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.090
of working as a secret agent on the planet.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:22.520
But as the film goes on, the line between
fact and fiction becomes blurred, and Quaid

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:26.720
struggles to figure out who he really is and
what he has really done on Mars.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.700
"If I'm not me, who the hell am I?"

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:35.160
Here's where the 2012 remake went completely
wrong: it isn't even set on Mars.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.960
Instead, it takes place on a future, dystopian
Earth.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:42.220
And sure, that concept could have worked well
anyway, but this version of Total Recall fell

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.840
flat in pretty much every other aspect, too.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:48.420
"If I'm not me, then who the hell am I?"

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.040
According to some critics, the action sequences
were the only real bright spot of the film.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:54.980
CineVue's Joseph Walsh wrote:

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.820
"Whilst the action is grand in scope and certainly
high in production values it somehow lacks

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.130
any tangible sense of tension or drama."

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:08.010
In John Carpenter's 1980 cult classic The
Fog, a mysterious fog covers a California

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:12.140
town, bringing with it the ghosts of dead
sailors who promptly set about terrorizing

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:12.900
the residents.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:17.040
The concept could have been corny, but the
film's slow build to its scarier moments kept

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:17.900
audiences hooked.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:22.100
It seemed like a film that could have benefited
from a remake with updated special effects

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:22.600
too.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.260
Sadly, the 2005 remake managed to botch it
completely.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.520
Although Carpenter produced the remake himself,
it just didn't strike a chord with critics.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:34.760
The scares weren't scary, and the ghosts didn't
come across as threatening or intimidating

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:35.640
in any way.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.780
And if a horror film isn't genuinely scary,
it usually ends up feeling boring and muddled

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:42.580
which is exactly what happened here.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.050
Writing for ScreenCrush, critic Matt Singer
suggested it could be one of the worst movies

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:47.760
ever made.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:48.540
He wrote:

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:50.620
"This movie's not even that foggy!

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.230
The clouds in two-thirds of this movie are
so sparse they wouldn't even warrant a push

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:56.200
notification from your phone's weather app."

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:02.240
The Stepfather is a 1987 slasher about a murderer
who kills his family, changes his identity,

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:05.530
and then marries into another family so he
can repeat the process.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:09.590
His new stepdaughter soon becomes suspicious
of him, and sets out to prove his identity

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:10.540
and save her family.

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:15.220
The film falls somewhere between horror, crime
thriller, and black comedy, and the plot is

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:19.900
just interesting enough to carry audiences
along to a genuinely satisfying conclusion.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:23.520
But the 2009 remake of The Stepfather failed
to do the same.

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:28.620
The original's palpable tension just isn't
there, the plot holes are distracting, and

00:03:28.629 --> 00:03:32.970
the climax of the film ties up everything
a little too neatly with a predictable series

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:35.459
of events leading up the final moments.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.590
For the AV Club, Scott Tobias wrote:

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:41.840
"Even by horror-remake standards, The Stepfather
sets the bar for pointlessness."

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:47.980
Originally released in 1956, Around the World
in 80 Days is an epic adventure based on the

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:52.340
novel of the same name by Jules Verne one
that ended up winning five Academy Awards,

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:54.140
including the award for Best Picture.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.330
The plot is pretty simple: Phileas Fogg makes
a bet that he can get around the world in

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:01.840
a mere eighty days, and subsequently sets
off on a race across the globe.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:07.220
A tale like this might seem timeless, but
the 2004 remake didn't take audiences on quite

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:08.440
the same ride as the original.

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:13.590
Instead, it barely followed the plot of Verne's
novel, and ended up being nominated for Worst

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:14.680
Remake at the Razzies.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.040
It also went on to win Most Unwelcome Remake
at The Stinkers Bad Movie Awards.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.520
Marty Mapes summed it up for Movie Habit,
when he wrote:

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:25.319
"Around the World in 80 Days is the kind of
movie you should see if you're looking for

00:04:25.319 --> 00:04:28.819
an excuse to sit in an air-conditioned theater
for two hours, and you've already seen all

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:29.900
the good movies."

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:34.760
Nicolas Cage has a strong track record of
starring in films that prove a little underwhelming

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:38.220
for critics, and Bangkok Dangerous definitely
falls into that category.

00:04:38.909 --> 00:04:43.199
The original Bangkok Dangerous is a Thai crime
thriller about Kong, a gunman who can neither

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:44.040
hear nor speak.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:48.610
He works as an assassin for hire, struggles
to find real meaning in life, and eventually

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:49.760
meets a tragic end.

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:52.560
It's an intense film, packed full of action
and emotion.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.099
Cage's version, however, couldn't hold a candle
to the original despite the fact that it was

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:58.440
made by the same directors.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:02.960
In the 2008 remake of Bangkok Dangerous, several
key details are changed.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:06.480
Cage plays the protagonist, Joe, who isn't
deaf or mute.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:10.879
The cinematography is visually confusing at
times, and the idea of a hitman who eventually

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.400
gains a conscience feels kinda cliche.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.370
As Scott Nash remarked in a review for Three
Movie Buffs:

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:19.260
"The burnt out assassin shtick is old and
tired."

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:24.240
The 1959 historical drama Ben-Hur is an undisputed
classic.

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:28.860
Interestingly enough, this version of Ben-Hur
was actually a remake itself, and one which

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:33.620
definitely improved on the original 1925 silent
film, Ben-Hur: A Tale of the Christ.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.400
For one thing, it features Hollywood's most
iconic chariot race.

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:42.539
It was difficult to see how a remake could
have outdone the 1959 version but that didn't

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:44.200
stop Paramount from trying.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:48.560
What could have been a stunning historical
epic turned out to be a box office bomb.

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:53.740
The aesthetics were downgraded by haphazard
editing and lackluster CGI, and aside from

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:57.040
a few exciting action scenes, little about
the film stood out.

00:05:57.160 --> 00:05:59.080
Alison Rowat wrote in The Herald:

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:03.220
"A thrill ride in parts, as long as you can
forgive the hokiness, wooden dialogue, and

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:04.660
long-slog running time."

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:11.119
1956's Invasion of the Body Snatchers might
not seem as slick or pretty as today's horror

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:13.520
films, but it's still widely regarded as a
classic.

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:18.100
In this sci-fi horror movie, alien spores
grow into seed pods that can produce physical

00:06:18.119 --> 00:06:21.940
replacements of human beings without any shred
of human emotions.

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:27.009
A critically and commercially successful remake
followed in 1978, and another retelling, titled

00:06:27.009 --> 00:06:29.520
Body Snatchers, was released in 1993.

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:34.660
In 2007, another version of the story was
released, this time titled The Invasion.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:38.960
Rather than creating a straight remake, the
writers tried to take it in a different direction

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:41.560
and make the story more contemporary and political.

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:42.280
The result?

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:47.360
A film that was widely criticized for an inconsistent
narrative, one whose deeper themes were subsequently

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:48.520
lost in the mess.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.300
For Slate, Dana Stevens wrote:

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:54.879
"It falls far short as an effective sci-fi
thriller, not to mention the brainy political

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:56.620
allegory it's determined to be."

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:02.900
1953's House of Wax was a 3-D horror hit in
which a sculptor stocks his museum by killing

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:04.960
people and coating their corpses with wax.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:09.820
It was actually a remake of an earlier film,
the 1933 Mystery of the Wax Museum.

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:14.000
Audiences were mostly enthusiastic about this
version, and it did fairly well at the box

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:14.500
office.

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:16.300
All in all, not a bad go.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:21.500
In 2005, a modern-day House of Wax was released,
but the plot was quite different, preferring

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:24.260
to err more on the "shrill teen slasher" side
of things.

00:07:24.589 --> 00:07:29.679
Now, there's nothing wrong with a cheesy slasher
but compared to the impact of the 1953 version,

00:07:29.679 --> 00:07:33.319
this retelling was shallow and way too reliant
on cheap scares.

00:07:33.319 --> 00:07:37.809
Granted, this was probably obvious based on
the casting alone: with Paris Hilton in a

00:07:37.809 --> 00:07:40.779
supporting role, it was never going to be
taking home an Oscar.

00:07:40.779 --> 00:07:42.699
As Maitland McDonagh wrote for TV Guide:

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:47.920
"It delivers some bracingly nasty gore scenes,
but there's no spark left in the run-scream-repeat

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:48.420
formula."

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:54.759
In the 1991 film Point Break, Keanu Reeves
plays an undercover FBI agent who has to investigate

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:58.260
and infiltrate a group of bank robbers who
also happen to be surfers.

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:02.259
But while the premise of Point Break may have
seemed clunky, everything still managed to

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:06.260
come together to make a downright ridiculous
yet genuinely entertaining movie.

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:17.099
So how do you recapture the magic of a film
with an unexpected cult following?

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:21.320
Well, if the movie's 2015 remake is any indication…
you can't.

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:26.740
The 2015 Point Break features some visually
stunning action scenes, but other than that,

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:27.860
it totally falls flat.

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:32.930
In a review for Times of India, Reagan Gavin
Rasquinha summed up the film's fatal flaw:

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:37.880
"Point Break comes across as a string of admittedly
amazing action sequences and sports feats

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:40.780
with the rest of the film haphazardly built
up around it."

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:46.360
The 1985 zombie film Day of the Dead follows
a group of scientists and soldiers who live

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:48.860
in a bunker in the aftermath of a zombie invasion.

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:53.500
Several zombies are kept captive for the purpose
of research; an arrangement that obviously

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:55.040
goes very wrong indeed.

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:59.880
Before long, a conflict breaks out that threatens
the very survival of the human race.

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:05.420
Now, Day of the Dead is obviously a pretty
bloody film, but as with all of George Romero's

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:08.740
zombie movies, it balances
its violence with a genuinely thoughtful critique

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:09.360
of society.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:14.520
However, the 2008 remake forsakes any attempt
to bring a real message to the audience in

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:16.180
favor of gore and guts.

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:20.080
There's no real commentary on the state of
humanity, the special effects look cheap,

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:24.440
and the zombies are so intelligent and agile
that they operate less like zombies and more

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:26.700
like evil human beings with superpowers.

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:29.820
Writing for the Pittsburgh City Paper, Jordan
Snowden said:

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:34.620
"Like infected zombie areas put under quarantine,
I would lock this film away in a box and leave

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:35.620
it there for good."

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:42.220
Adam Sandler's 2002 remake of Frank Capra's
1936 film Mr. Deeds Goes to Town doesn't exactly

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:43.800
rank amongst his best work.

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:48.300
In the original, Longfellow Deeds gets by
making a living by juggling odd jobs during

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:52.690
the Great Depression, until he suddenly inherits
$20 million from his late uncle.

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:57.260
He soon falls in love with Louise "Babe" Bennett,
and an unexpected love story unfolds.

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:02.260
The plot of 2002's Mr. Deeds is similar, but
the jokes are grating, the characters are

00:10:02.270 --> 00:10:06.570
bland and irritating, and the entire narrative
is dumbed down to the point where it's practically

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:09.500
insulting to the audience and to Capra's memory.

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:12.410
In a review for the Seattle Times, Moira Macdonald
wrote:

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:16.600
"Mr. Deeds is supposed to be a celebration
of the goodness of regular people... but really

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:20.300
it's nothing more than a money maker for the
filmmakers and a pointless star vehicle for

00:10:20.300 --> 00:10:20.880
Sandler."

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:25.720
1972's The Heartbreak Kid is a different kind
of romantic comedy.

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:30.460
Self-absorbed Lenny is married to clingy Lila,
and on their honeymoon, he ditches her to

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:32.980
pursue a manipulative college girl named Kelly.

00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:37.540
He impulsively divorces Lila and proposes
to Kelly, but it becomes clear that her sole

00:10:37.540 --> 00:10:40.000
reason for marrying him is to rebel against
her father.

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:44.680
At their wedding, Lenny ends up ignored by
almost everyone, including the bride, and

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:47.340
this dark comedy ends on a fairly depressing
note.

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:52.690
The 2007 remake of The Heartbreak Kid, starring
Ben Stiller, didn't do quite so much to push

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:54.080
the boundaries of the genre.

00:10:54.490 --> 00:10:58.720
The characters are nasty to the point of being
practically irredeemable, and the whole thing

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:01.930
totally fails to differentiate itself from
any other film in the genre.

00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:05.440
In fact, Rolling Stone dubbed it the worst
remake of the year.

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:08.810
In a review for eFilmCritic, Peter Sobczynski
said:

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:12.920
"[The Heartbreak Kid is] an ugly, hateful
and deeply unfunny bit of hackwork."

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:19.440
Remaking a truly classic horror film is no
easy task, and the 1998 remake of Psycho made

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:20.540
that crystal clear.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:25.450
It certainly didn't help that the original
1960 Psycho was downright chilling and near-perfect

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:28.780
— so fans weren't exactly begging for a
do-over, either.

00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:31.360
"We all go a little mad sometimes."

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:36.120
The remake didn't take much artistic license,
opting instead for a shot-for-shot copy of

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:36.660
the original.

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:40.780
Honestly, it's hard not to ask why it was
worth making the film in the first place if

00:11:40.780 --> 00:11:43.680
the filmmakers would refuse to deviate from
the source material.

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:47.740
Everything from the dialogue to the score
is almost a carbon copy of the original.

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:49.920
Somehow, though, they still got it wrong.

00:11:50.420 --> 00:11:54.910
The film was nominated for a grand total of
three Golden Raspberry Awards, and won for

00:11:54.910 --> 00:11:56.640
Worst Remake and Worst Director.

00:11:57.020 --> 00:11:59.520
Nitrate Online Review's Sean Axmaker wrote:

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:01.900
"Even with Hitchcock's shot list...

00:12:01.910 --> 00:12:05.220
Van Sant can't come up with anything more
than a wan tribute to the master, proving

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:07.840
it takes more than a good storyboard to make
a film work."

00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:13.760
In 2006, the Japanese horror film Kairo was
remade for American audiences as Pulse.

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:18.340
In Kairo, evil spirits manage to find their
way into our world through the internet, causing

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:20.860
a series of strange and horrifying events
to occur.

00:12:21.430 --> 00:12:25.540
The film features two parallel narratives
that show characters dealing with the consequences

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:27.000
of this paranormal invasion.

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:30.200
In the remake, the unique dual narrative approach
was scrapped.

00:12:30.500 --> 00:12:34.160
The premise was similar enough, but everything
that made the original a cult favorite in

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:35.400
Japan was missing.

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:39.700
The film relies on grotesque imagery to get
its points across, but those cheap thrills

00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:43.500
aren't enough for a film that fails to delve
deeper into any meaningful themes.

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:46.920
Writing for the Austin Chronicle, critic Mark
Savlov said:

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:52.080
"[The remake is a] curiously dull Americanization
of one of the finest examples of subtle, moody

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:52.920
J-horror out there."

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:57.660
The 1980 film Fame follows a group of high
school students after they gain acceptance

00:12:57.660 --> 00:13:00.620
to the prestigious High School of Performing
Arts in New York City.

00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:05.020
As they study their respective crafts, they
face difficult obstacles in the classroom,

00:13:05.020 --> 00:13:06.640
on stage, and in their personal lives.

00:13:06.970 --> 00:13:10.640
There are moments that slip into melodrama,
but overall, the characters are relatable

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:12.960
and sympathetic, and the musical numbers were
a hit.

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:17.000
But the 2009 remake of Fame just doesn't strike
that same atmosphere.

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:21.380
It's too slick and polished, like it was intended
to be an after-school special rather than

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:24.140
a film about authentic, complex, and ambitious
characters.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:29.020
It lacks the original's gritty edge too, seeming
to take cues from High School Musical and

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:29.920
Glee instead.

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:32.900
Critic Roger Ebert gave the film two stars,
writing:

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:37.500
"The new Fame is a sad reflection of the new
Hollywood, where material is sanitized and

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:41.220
dumbed down for a hypothetical teen market
that is way too sophisticated for it."

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:47.500
1973 British horror film The Wicker Man follows
devout Christian police sergeant Neil Howie

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:49.980
as he investigates an isolated island cult.

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:54.830
He travels to the island of Summerisle to
find a missing young girl, Rowan Morrison,

00:13:54.830 --> 00:13:58.680
who he believes has been taken to the island
based on the contents of an anonymous letter.

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:03.060
Howie worries that she's intended to be a
human sacrifice for the cult, but what they

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:05.500
have planned for Howie is even more terrifying.

00:14:06.220 --> 00:14:11.000
The Nicolas Cage-led 2006 remake of The Wicker
Man couldn't help but fall short of the eerie

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:12.740
expectations set by the original.

00:14:13.060 --> 00:14:17.580
Moments intended to be harrowing are instead
so absurd that they came across as genuinely

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:21.800
hilarious, and the unsettling atmosphere of
the original film is completely missing.

00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:28.880
“Not the bees [...] Not the bees ah. my
eyes, my eyes!”

00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:32.880
When a horror movie comes off more like an
ill-conceived comedy, it's hard to walk away

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:35.670
without feeling like everyone involved was
phoning it in.

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:41.210
The 2008 American remake of the Japanese film
One Missed Call has the distinction of being

00:14:41.210 --> 00:14:45.080
one of the few films to score a whopping zero
percent on the Tomatometer.

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:49.480
The original One Missed Call wasn't exactly
a classic, but this just meant there was a

00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:52.320
real opportunity for the remake to outshine
the original.

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:54.520
And it still completely missed the mark.

00:14:54.790 --> 00:14:58.820
One Missed Call just wasn't scary enough for
critics, but that lack of fear factor isn't

00:14:58.820 --> 00:15:00.060
the movie's real problem.

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:03.680
No, the real problem is that it's just so
unforgivably boring.

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:06.210
As Common Sense Media wrote in their review:

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:10.760
"One Missed Call suffers from predictable
characters, overused conventions of the horror

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:13.240
genre, and a plot that never really makes
sense."

00:15:13.900 --> 00:15:18.400
The 1939 comedy film The Women featured an
all-female cast, and while one of the main

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:22.800
themes of the film is the characters' relationships
with men, not a single man is seen throughout.

00:15:23.410 --> 00:15:27.440
With today's push for more female representation
in movies, you would think that remaking The

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:30.220
Women would present a great opportunity for
Hollywood.

00:15:30.500 --> 00:15:33.480
Sadly, the 2008 remake left much to be desired.

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:38.400
It wasn't anywhere near as witty or charming
as the original, and the ensemble cast's talent

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:39.800
was largely wasted.

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:44.300
Comparing the film to the 1939 version, critic
Linda Barnard wrote for The Star:

00:15:44.540 --> 00:15:49.180
"What was then snappy dialogue from meowing
madams [...] now flaccidly flaps, lost in

00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:51.260
translation from old world to new."

